    <node id="Maximum path lengths, complexity per layer and sequential operations for different layer types (table)">
      <data key="d0">Maximum path lengths, complexity per layer and sequential operations for different layer types (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table compares the computational complexity, sequential operations, and maximum path lengths for different types of layers used in the model. Self-Attention has a complexity of O(n² ·d), Recurrent has O(n·d²), Convolutional has O(k ·n·d²), and Self-Attention (restricted) has O(r ·n·d). All layers except Self-Attention (restricted) have a complexity of O(1), while Self-Attention (restricted) has a complexity of O(1). Sequential operations are O(1) for all layers except Self-Attention (restricted), which is O(1). The maximum path length is O(1) for all layers except Self-Attention (restricted), which is O(n/r). This table supports the surrounding content by illustrating the trade-offs between computational complexity and path length in different types of layers, the which is a key factor in the design of the model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d37284483d5c1e39cf1b5866536aa92</data>
      <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1763824580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Positional Encoding Equation (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Positional Encoding Equation (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">The equation represents the positional encoding for a given position pos and dimensionality d_model. It uses sine and cosine functions to encode the position relative to the model's dimensionality. The frequency of the sine and cosine functions is determined by the position pos and the model's dimensionality d_model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2a7228dd2b90dc5139aae7e2e8cc6b</data>
      <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1763824580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Rate Schedule for Adam Optimizer (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Learning Rate Schedule for Adam Optimizer (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation provides a flexible and adaptive learning rate schedule for training deep learning models, particularly in the field of machine translation. It is used to improve the performance of the transformer model by adjusting the learning rate based on the model's dimensionality and the current step number in the training process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29a243014df975538b05b5d31733733c</data>
      <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1763824580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 2: BLEU scores and training costs for different models (table)">
      <data key="d0">Table 2: BLEU scores and training costs for different models (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table compares the BLEU scores and training costs for various models, including ByteNet, Deep-Att + PosUnk, GNMT + RL, ConvS2S, MoE, Deep-Att + PosUnk Ensemble, GNMT +RL Ensemble, ConvS2S Ensemble, Transformer (base model), and Transformer (big). The Transformer (big) model achieves the highest BLEU scores and has the lowest training costs, indicating that more complex models can achieve better performance with lower training costs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ec23fcb7fe3ccfb7197a4109b7c438d</data>
      <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1763824580</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computational Resources for Training Deep Learning Models (discarded)">
      <data key="d0">Computational Resources for Training Deep Learning Models (discarded)</data>
      <data key="d1">discarded</data>
      <data key="d2">The discarded content provides specific values for the theoretical maximum floating-point operations per second (TFLOPS) for various NVIDIA GPUs, including K80, K40, M40, and P100. This information is crucial for understanding the computational efficiency and cost-effectiveness of different GPU models in training deep learning models, particularly in the context of machine translation tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c4afcaa29b888e5dd2e4da94a3adaf9</data>
